1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,624
I'm Shivam Gupta, founder of Pactshift.

2
00:00:03,784 --> 00:00:06,846
An agency agrees to build a website.

3
00:00:07,006 --> 00:00:10,942
Halfway through, the client asks for one
more thing.

4
00:00:11,102 --> 00:00:15,913
The request changes, but the budget and
delivery plan often don't.

5
00:00:16,073 --> 00:00:21,322
Pactshift turns that conversation into a
choice everyone can see, and an

6
00:00:21,482 --> 00:00:23,669
agreement the project actually follows.

7
00:00:24,369 --> 00:00:28,305
Let's take a real workflow using fictional
sample data.

8
00:00:28,465 --> 00:00:32,401
Northstar Studio is building this website
for Forma Architecture.

9
00:00:32,561 --> 00:00:35,623
The fixed budget is twelve thousand
dollars.

10
00:00:35,783 --> 00:00:38,845
The agreed work totals ninety-six
estimated hours.

11
00:00:39,005 --> 00:00:44,253
Now the client wants Spanish-language
pages, estimated at twelve hours, with the

12
00:00:44,413 --> 00:00:47,037
translated copy supplied by the client.

13
00:00:47,737 --> 00:00:50,799
The request sits beside the saved scope.

14
00:00:50,959 --> 00:00:56,207
The analysis helps the owner review what
is changing, and the interface

15
00:00:56,367 --> 00:00:58,554
shows which engine produced it.

16
00:00:58,714 --> 00:01:03,963
AI can assist here, but it does not decide
what the client

17
00:01:04,123 --> 00:01:04,560
owes.

18
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:06,907
The owner confirms the effort.

19
00:01:07,067 --> 00:01:12,315
The server calculates the fee from the
project's rate: twelve hours at

20
00:01:12,475 --> 00:01:16,412
one hundred and twenty-five dollars is
fifteen hundred dollars.

21
00:01:17,112 --> 00:01:19,299
Charging more is one option.

22
00:01:19,459 --> 00:01:24,707
But the client may care more about the
launch date and a

23
00:01:24,867 --> 00:01:25,742
fixed budget.

24
00:01:25,902 --> 00:01:28,963
So the agency can offer an exchange.

25
00:01:29,123 --> 00:01:33,935
This resource library is planned for
sixteen hours and hasn't started.

26
00:01:34,095 --> 00:01:38,031
We can replace it with the twelve-hour
language addition.

27
00:01:38,191 --> 00:01:41,690
Work already underway, locked
deliverables, and dependencies constrain

28
00:01:41,850 --> 00:01:43,599
what can be offered.

29
00:01:44,299 --> 00:01:48,673
Now I'll share that proposal and open the
client view.

30
00:01:48,833 --> 00:01:52,332
The client doesn't need to create an
account.

31
00:01:52,492 --> 00:01:57,741
They can see the addition with its fee and
schedule impact, the

32
00:01:57,901 --> 00:02:01,399
exact deliverable exchange, or the option
to defer.

33
00:02:01,559 --> 00:02:03,746
They also see the assumptions.

34
00:02:03,906 --> 00:02:08,280
There is no surprise invoice hiding behind
an approval button.

35
00:02:08,980 --> 00:02:14,229
For this demonstration, I'll act as the
fictional client and choose the

36
00:02:14,389 --> 00:02:14,826
exchange.

37
00:02:14,986 --> 00:02:19,797
I enter a name, acknowledge the terms, and
confirm the choice.

38
00:02:20,497 --> 00:02:25,745
Back in the agency workspace, this is the
important part: the agreement

39
00:02:25,905 --> 00:02:26,343
changed.

40
00:02:26,503 --> 00:02:30,877
The resource library has been exchanged
for the Spanish-language pages.

41
00:02:31,037 --> 00:02:34,098
The budget is still twelve thousand
dollars.

42
00:02:34,258 --> 00:02:36,445
The delivery date is unchanged.

43
00:02:36,605 --> 00:02:41,854
Total estimated work moves from ninety-six
to ninety-two hours, and the new

44
00:02:42,014 --> 00:02:43,763
baseline records the decision.

45
00:02:44,463 --> 00:02:49,711
That is four hours of planned capacity
preserved, not four hours of

46
00:02:49,871 --> 00:02:50,309
revenue.

47
00:02:50,469 --> 00:02:55,280
The system also keeps previous baselines
and a linked decision history.

48
00:02:55,440 --> 00:02:59,814
Version checks protect against stale
approvals, and repeated acceptance cannot

49
00:02:59,974 --> 00:03:01,723
duplicate the new work.

50
00:03:01,883 --> 00:03:04,945
The owner can export the workspace record.

51
00:03:05,105 --> 00:03:09,916
The client acknowledgement records a
decision; it does not independently verify

52
00:03:10,076 --> 00:03:11,388
the signer's identity.

53
00:03:12,088 --> 00:03:16,899
Pactshift is built for small agencies that
need this conversation handled

54
00:03:17,059 --> 00:03:17,496
clearly.

55
00:03:17,656 --> 00:03:22,905
The proposed subscriptions start with a
free plan, then twenty-nine dollars for

56
00:03:23,065 --> 00:03:27,439
Studio and seventy-nine dollars for
Agency, with defined stored-project and

57
00:03:27,599 --> 00:03:28,473
analysis allowances.

58
00:03:28,633 --> 00:03:33,882
Client review has no seat charge, and
Pactshift takes no cut of

59
00:03:34,042 --> 00:03:35,791
an agency's approved work.

60
00:03:36,491 --> 00:03:41,302
This is a pre-validation product, with no
claimed customers or revenue.

61
00:03:41,462 --> 00:03:46,711
I developed Pactshift with substantial AI
assistance, and the next step is

62
00:03:46,871 --> 00:03:51,245
testing this workflow with real agency
owners and their clients.

63
00:03:51,405 --> 00:03:56,653
The goal is simple: when the request
changes, help both sides agree

64
00:03:56,813 --> 00:03:59,000
on what changes with it.
